ref('maeta2026regularized','Maeta, Reishi','Regularized master-field approximation for large-{$N$} reduced matrix models',2026,'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/JHEP08(2026)125',journal='Journal of High Energy Physics',volume='2026',number=8,pages='125',doi='10.1007/JHEP08(2026)125',annote='Publisher full PDF opened, abstract and section 5 read. Preprint arXiv:2605.10720; published 14 August 2026. One- and two-Hermitian-matrix numerical examples; Eguchi--Kawai models proposed as future applications. Does not supply the four-unitary global free energy.')
talk(565,'1982-09-20','A. A. Migdal','P001037-P001041','Stochastic quantization and reduction to large random matrices',r'The mathematical entry starts on the lower half of P001038 after Sinai and ends on P001040. It develops Langevin quantization, factorization of normalized traces, quenched momenta and reduction of a matrix field to finitely many matrices. The definite calculation requested is the all-coupling large-\(N\) free energy of four unitary matrices. The manuscript retains a negative exponent and all ordered index pairs; those conventions are essential to the development. The one-matrix external-field integral at the end is a separate already studied model.')
add(565,'Planar counting and the matrix-field action','P001037-P001038','method',r'The agenda states \(u_\infty=sp_\infty=O_\infty\). Migdal considers a \(U(N)\)-valued or \(\mathfrak u(N)\)-valued field, action \(S=(N/\lambda)\int\operatorname{tr}[\tfrac12(\partial\varphi)^2+U(\varphi)]\), and large-\(N\) non-Gaussian fluctuations.',r'For Hermitian matrix fields with a stable regularized Euclidean action and fixed coupling, ribbon-graph perturbation theory organizes connected vacuum diagrams by Euler characteristic: an orientable genus-\(g\) diagram contributes \(N^{2-2g}\). Orthogonal and symplectic matrix theories also have nonorientable corrections. For anti-Hermitian fields a positive kinetic form is \(-\operatorname{tr}(\partial\varphi)^2\), so the printed sign requires a convention change.',r'\citet[Sections 2--3]{thooft1974planar}, received in December 1973 and published in 1974, establishes the diagrammatic expansion. The agenda equality is at most a leading-planar analogy, not an isomorphism of infinite classical Lie algebras.',('thooft1974planar',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',reconstruction='A regulator, stable action, Hermitian convention and perturbative meaning are specified; the three classical series are not literally identified.')
add(565,'Langevin equilibrium and stochastic quantization','P001039','method',r'The equation \(\partial_\tau\varphi+\delta S/\delta\varphi=J\) is assigned unit white-noise covariance and is claimed to reproduce \(Z^{-1}\int Qe^{-S}D\varphi\) by a long-time average.',r'For a finite regulator, \(dX=-\nabla S(X)\,d\tau+\sqrt2\,dB_\tau\) has invariant density \(Z^{-1}e^{-S}\) when \(Z<\infty\); ergodicity is additionally needed for almost-sure time averages. Unit noise covariance instead gives density proportional to \(e^{-2S}\). A continuum limit requires separate construction and renormalization.',r'The Parisi--Wu method was published in 1981 and is the explicit starting point of \citet[abstract]{alfarosakita1983quenched}. Here the normalization is checked directly from the finite-dimensional stationary Fokker--Planck equation. No construction of four-dimensional continuum Yang--Mills measure follows.',('alfarosakita1983quenched',),literal='false as written',verification='indirect',reconstruction='The finite-dimensional statement and direct equilibrium check are editorial; the historical identification relies on the later paper abstract.')
add(565,'Large-N factorization and the meaning of self-averaging','P001039','claim',r'For \(G=N^{-1}\operatorname{tr}(\varphi(x_1)\cdots\varphi(x_n))\), the notes assert self-averaging up to \(O(1/N)\), after taking stochastic time large.',r'In the planar expansion of a regularized invariant matrix theory, connected correlators of two normalized single traces are \(O(N^{-2})\). When this estimate holds uniformly for the actual probability measure, standard deviations are \(O(N^{-1})\). Planar graph counting alone does not establish uniform stochastic-time convergence or an exchange of the limits \(N\to\infty\), \(\tau\to\infty\), and cutoff removal.',r'The topological mechanism is in \citet[Sections 2--3]{thooft1974planar}; factorization is used in \citet[abstract]{eguchikawai1982reduction}, published April 1982. The qualification between formal expansion and a measure theorem is necessary.',('thooft1974planar','eguchikawai1982reduction'),verification='read the result',reconstruction='The distinction between covariance and standard deviation and between formal and convergent limits is added.')
add(565,'Quenched momenta and conjugated noise','P001039-P001040','construction',r'Diagonal random momenta \(p_\mu\) are introduced, with \(\varphi(x)=e^{ip\cdot x}\bar\varphi e^{-ip\cdot x}\) and similarly conjugated matrix noise. The density is \(e^{-p^2/\Lambda^2}\), said to have variance \(1/\Lambda^2\).',r'In a regulated planar expansion, color-index momentum differences \(p_i-p_j\) represent line momenta. Averaging sampled diagonal momenta with a specified cutoff density approximates the momentum integrals. For one real coordinate with normalized density proportional to \(e^{-p^2/\Lambda^2}\), the variance is \(\Lambda^2/2\). Conjugated finite-matrix noise is not entrywise spacetime white noise as an exact finite-\(N\) identity.',r'\citet[abstract]{alfarosakita1983quenched} gives the same stochastic route; its July 1982 preprint preceded this September talk, although publication followed in February 1983. This identifies an expository method, without certifying a general nonperturbative equivalence.',('alfarosakita1983quenched',),literal='false as written',match='exact',verification='indirect',reconstruction='The planar and cutoff meaning is made explicit; the elementary Gaussian variance is corrected.')
add(565,'The reduced Langevin equation and formal matrix measure','P001040','construction',r'Equations (15)--(18) replace spatial derivatives by commutators with \(p_\mu\), write \(U(\varphi)\) in the drift, and then integrate \(e^{+\operatorname{tr}([p,\varphi]^2+U_\Lambda(\varphi))}\) over a set labeled \(U(N)\), without a partition-function denominator.',r'If \(\varphi(x)=e^{ipx}\Phi e^{-ipx}\), then \(-\partial^2\varphi=e^{ipx}\sum_\mu[p_\mu,[p_\mu,\Phi]]e^{-ipx}\). The drift from \(\operatorname{tr}U(\Phi)\) contains \(U^{\prime}(\Phi)\). A reduced expectation must specify whether \(\Phi\) is Hermitian, anti-Hermitian or unitary, a stable action, a measure on that domain, normalization, and the separate averaging of quenched momenta.',r'The stochastic prescription is documented by \citet{alfarosakita1983quenched}; the speaker published the related QCD reduction in \citet[pp.~425--428]{migdal1982reduction} later in 1982. Only institutional metadata and abstracts were accessible, so the exact displayed equations are not certified as the published result.',('alfarosakita1983quenched','migdal1982reduction'),cl='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction='The displayed commutator identity is checked directly. The action derivative, domain and probability normalization are repairs, not silent replacements of the manuscript.',status='a historical reduction program is identified; the notebook equations themselves do not define the claimed probability measure.')
add(565,'All-coupling free energy of four unitary matrices','P001040','problem',r'Equation (19) asks for \(\lim_{N\to\infty}N^{-2}\log\int\exp[-\beta N\sum_{\mu,\nu=1}^4\operatorname{tr}(U_\mu U_\nu U_\mu^\dagger U_\nu^\dagger)]\prod_{\mu=1}^4dU_\mu\). The minus sign and the complete ordered double sum are visible in the scan.',r'With normalized Haar measures on \(U(N)\), set \(Z_N(\beta)=\int_{U(N)^4}\exp[-\beta N\sum_{\mu,\nu=1}^4\operatorname{tr}(U_\mu U_\nu U_\mu^\dagger U_\nu^\dagger)]\,dU\). Determine \(F(\beta)=\lim_{N\to\infty}N^{-2}\log Z_N(\beta)\) for real \(\beta\), including existence, analytic branches and transition values. The sum is real because ordered off-diagonal terms are conjugate pairs; diagonal terms contribute \(4N\).',r'\citet[Theorem~0.1]{collinsguionnetmaurel2009asymptotics}, first circulated in 2006 and published in 2009, gives convergence and analytic expansion for sufficiently small potential. The chapter computes the first two derivatives and \(F(\beta)=-4\beta+6\beta^2+O(\beta^3)\). The original \citet{eguchikawai1982reduction} uses different sign and summation conventions. Searches through November 2026 found no exact global solution for the displayed model; \citet[Section~5]{maeta2026regularized} remains a numerical small-model study and proposes larger reduced models for later work.',('collinsguionnetmaurel2009asymptotics','eguchikawai1982reduction','maeta2026regularized'),cl='C',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='read the result',problem='GP-1982-02',development='developed in this chapter',reconstruction='Normalized Haar measure, real coupling and the precise all-coupling objective are supplied. Diagonal terms and exponent sign are retained.',worth='Worth developing: a global characterization would connect a rigorously controlled local free energy with possible large-matrix phase transitions in a definite finite-dimensional integral.',status='small-coupling existence and expansion are established; a global all-coupling formula and phase description for the exact recorded convention were not located in the primary literature searched.',confidence='medium')
add(565,'The one-unitary external-field integral','P001040','claim',r'Equation (20) is \(\int_{U(N)}e^{\beta N\operatorname{tr}(Uh+h^\dagger U^\dagger)}dU\), with arbitrary \(h\). It is said to be solved by a name resembling \ru{Пиризен}, Gross, and then Fateev.',r'For fixed complex \(N\times N\) source \(h\), unitary invariance reduces the normalized Haar integral to the singular values of \(h\). The large-\(N\) external-field problem requires a specified limiting distribution and scaling of these singular values; it is distinct from the interacting four-matrix integral.',r'\citet[pp.~120--124]{brezingross1980external}, published November 1980, is the precise Brézin--Gross identification. The opened author bibliography and publisher abstract support the large-\(N\) match; they do not verify an arbitrary finite-\(N\) closed formula or the unidentified Fateev extension.',('brezingross1980external',),match='exact',reconstruction='The likely name identification is editorial. The finite-\(N\) reduction to singular values follows from Haar invariance.',confidence='medium')
add(565,'The unspecified Fateev extension of the external-field solution','P001040','remark',r'The final sentence adds \ru{затем Фатеев} without title, formula, parameter restriction or reference.',r'The note does not distinguish a finite-\(N\) formula, a new asymptotic regime, or a different external-field model. It therefore does not determine a separate mathematical claim attributable to Fateev.',r'The Brézin--Gross primary bibliographic match was checked, together with searches for Fateev and the unitary external-field integral. No exact Fateev publication matching this abbreviated sentence was verified.',('brezingross1980external',),cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',status='the attribution remains unidentified; it is not entered as an open problem.')
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1982-02',title='Global free energy of the recorded four-unitary matrix integral',statement=r'For normalized Haar measure, determine the large-\(N\) free energy of \(\exp[-\beta N\sum_{\mu,\nu=1}^4\operatorname{tr}(U_\mu U_\nu U_\mu^\dagger U_\nu^\dagger)]\) for all real coupling, with the full ordered sum and negative exponent retained.',occurrences=['1982-565.6'],status=r'As of November 2026, rigorous small-potential theory supplies a local analytic limit; the chapter computes \(F(\beta)=-4\beta+6\beta^2+O(\beta^3)\). The all-coupling formula and transition structure for this precise convention were not located. This is a bounded search conclusion, not a claim that no relevant unpublished solution exists.',status_date='2026-11',refs=['collinsguionnetmaurel2009asymptotics','eguchikawai1982reduction','maeta2026regularized']))
corr('P001038','Euclidean matrix action and real form',r'Anti-Hermitian \(\mathfrak u(N)\) field with positive \(\operatorname{tr}(\partial\varphi)^2\).',r'Choose Hermitian matrix fields, or use the positive form \(-\operatorname{tr}(AB)\) on anti-Hermitian matrices.','Russian and scan1038 inspected. For nonzero anti-Hermitian A, tr(A squared) is negative. This is a sign convention repair.')
corr('P001039','White-noise covariance and Gibbs weight',r'Unit covariance with drift \(-\nabla S\) and stationary weight \(e^{-S}\).',r'Use covariance \(2\delta\) for this drift, or halve the drift; otherwise the stationary weight is \(e^{-2S}\).','Russian and scan1039 inspected; finite-dimensional stationary Fokker--Planck equation checked.')
corr('P001040','Gaussian momentum variance',r'Density \(e^{-p^2/\Lambda^2}\), variance \(1/\Lambda^2\).',r'Variance \(\Lambda^2/2\) per real component for the written density.','Russian and scan1040 agree; direct Gaussian integral gives the variance.')
corr('P001040','Reduced drift in equation (16)',r'\(U(\varphi)\) in the Langevin drift.',r'\(U^{\prime}(\varphi)\) for the action potential named earlier.','Scan1040 inspected: no derivative is visible. Variation of tr U gives tr U prime delta phi.')
corr('P001040','Formal reduced expectation in equations (17)--(18)',r'Unnormalized integral over \(U(N)\) with the displayed positive exponent.',r'Specify the matrix integration domain and stable action convention, and divide by its partition function for an expectation.','Scan1040 and Russian inspected. Earlier phi is a Lie-algebra field, whereas U(N) denotes the compact group; the notebook does not resolve the change.')
corr('P001040','Name after external-field equation (20)',r'\ru{Пиризен} [uncertain first vowel], Gross; then Fateev.',r'Probable identification: Brézin--Gross (1980); retain uncertainty in the written surname and in the Fateev attribution.','Brézin author academy bibliography confirms the exact external-field paper with Gross. Handwriting does not justify silently replacing the ambiguous surname.',type='provenance')
audit.append('## Record 565\nActual pages: lower1038--1040. Russian fully read; scans1038,1039,1040 inspected, including Eq19 negative sign and full ordered sum. Independent checks: noise normalization, Gaussian variance, spatial double commutator, derivative U prime, and diagonal contribution4N. Opened primary sources: t Hooft author PDF (planar counting), Eguchi--Kawai APS abstract (received19Jan1982, published19Apr1982), Alfaro author CV entry3 and indexed publisher abstract (July1982 preprintCCNY-HEP-82/8, published10Feb1983), Landau1982 bibliography entries52/53 (Migdal distinct from Kazakov--Migdal), Brezin academy CV (external-field work). Original Migdal, Alfaro and Brezin full publisher PDFs inaccessible; only metadata/abstract verification claimed. Opened Collins--Guionnet--Maurel-Segala arXivmath/0608193 Theorem0.1. Current search included 2020 Neuberger PhysRevD102094503 fullPDF and 2026 Maeta JHEP08(2026)125 fullPDF section5: neither supplies exact four-unitary global free energy. Search terms: four-unitary Eguchi-Kawai free energy exact all coupling; reduced matrix model large N 2026; quenched momentum stochastic quantization. Neuberger discusses definitions of quenching and is not used to certify a global solution or its impossibility. Root independently develops GP-1982-02 at1982-565.6. Earlier index contains related loop and matrix methods but no exact integral problem. Parisi--Wu original full text could not be fetched; no invented full-result verification. Fateev extension remains F.')
